1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Alberta Edwards (nee Roon) of Gillette, N.J., died on May 22, 2010 following a long battle with leukemia. She was 83 years of age and a 52-year resident of Long Hill Township. Mrs. Edwards was born in 1926 in Lande
2 CONC shut in Silesia, which was then part of Germany and is now located in Poland. After living in several European countries during the years just before World War II, she emigrated to the USA in 1939. She attended several schools in 
2 CONC Europe and the US, and completed her secondary education at what is now Northfield Mount Hermon School in Massachusetts. She received her B.A. from Oberlin College in 1946, majoring in economics, and did graduate work at the New Y
2 CONC ork University Graduate School of Business. In 1980 Mrs. Edwards became the first woman vice president of the former Schering-Plough Corporation, which has now merged with Merck and Co., Inc., after beginning work there in 1956 as
2 CONC  a market analyst. For most of her career she worked in the International Division, retiring in 1992 as its vice president for public affairs. For many years she was a member of the board of trustees of Gill St. Bernard's School i
2 CONC n Gladstone, N.J., and was chair of the board's education committee for much of that time. She was also an active member throughout her adult life of the Appalachian Mountain Club and served on the board of that organization's Ech
2 CONC o Lake Camp in Southwest Harbor, Maine. Following her retirement from Schering-Plough, Mrs. Edwards joined the National Executive Service Corps, which provides voluntary assistance of retired business executives to non-profit orga
2 CONC nizations. She specialized in strategic planning, assisting many organizations, including Neighborhood House in Plainfield, N.J., Newark Emergency Services for Families, the Sturge-Weber Foundation, and several New Jersey public l
2 CONC ibraries. She also taught conversational English at schools and universities in Mexico, Italy, China, and Poland, working with Global Volunteers of St. Paul, Minn. Mrs. Edwards' most recent volunteer work was with Opportunity Inte
2 CONC rnational, a global organization committed to eradicating poverty around the world. Opportunity International provides micro-loans to low-income people and families in developing countries, and recipients use the funds to start or
2 CONC  expand businesses. Over several decades, the organization has assisted clients in more than 25 countries, providing over 10 million small loans, mostly to women, with a repayment rate of approximately 98 percent. Mrs. Edwards is 
2 CONC survived by her husband, Roger, of Gillette, N.J.; her children, Jeffrey, Julie, and Christopher; her sister, Leonore Laan; and eight grandchildren. She will be missed also by many friends and extended family members. A memorial s
2 CONC ervice will be held at 12.30 p.m. on Saturday, June 19, at Riverside Church's Christ Chapel, 490 Riverside Drive in New York City. The family requests that in lieu of flowers, donations be made in her memory to Opportunity Interna
2 CONC tional, at www.opportunity.org or 2122 York Road, Suite 150, Oak Brook, Ill. 60523. Published in Star-Ledger on June 6, 2010 - See more at: http://obits.nj.com/obituaries/starledger/obituary.aspx?pid=143347064#sthash.L7IwgqYr.dpuf
