2 DATE 16 OCT 1961
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Einar was landssekreterare (Attorney General) to the governor (landshövdingen)  i Uppland).  His was one of the two top political positions reporting directly to the governor of Uppland.  I belive (do you know more
2 CONC , Göran?) that he was in charge of the administration of  the health  and judicial government agencies, the other lateral position was the financial side. Before becoming landssekreterare Einar worked  during the tenure of Hjalmar
2 CONC  Hammarskjöld's governorship, 1907-1930. Hjalmar was the father of Dag Hammarskjöld and Einar's only son, Dag Svante, was named for Dag Hammarshkjöld.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his younger sister Helfrid, Einar suffered from depressions. He once went through a major depression, where he was away from work for months. Einar was a very silent, private man at the best of times, and my grandmother Signe
2 CONC  told me how after months of depression he got up one day, shaved, had breakfast and headed out of the door with his briefcase. Signe called his secretary at the Uppsala Castle where he had his office, and said, "Jag tror, att lan
2 CONC dssekreteraren är på väg till sitt arbete. (I believe that the  Attorney General is on his way to work)". The secretary reassured my grandmother that all would be ready for his arrival, and she and the rest of his staff greeted hi
2 CONC m as if nothing was out of the ordinary. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His treatment for depression had included trepanation, and it had affected his hearling, so that for the rest of his life, he had a very bothersome noise, "like a waterfall". (My cousin Göran Hoppe says that this story may be apoc
2 CONC ryphal: our grandfather told Göran that he (Einar) sufffered from Meunier's disease, and he may have had the surgery in an attempt to treat an ear infection.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT I have my story about what I guess was a lobotomy from my mother and grandmother and I cannot imagine that my grandfather would have mentioned that version to anybody. I am not even sure he would have known--he was oblivious to wh
2 CONC at went on in the family, totally focused on his work, music and travels. (He made many journeys alone on the continent.) My mother said that the children were  afraid of him, because of his aloofness, his uninvolvement with the f
2 CONC amily and his expectations of perfect, quiet, adult behavior. For instance, my mother who hated oatmeal, would be forced to walk around the table every morning, taking a spoonful every time she passed her chair. Eventually the res
2 CONC t of the family would leave the table, and the maids would take pity on her, take her out into the kitchen and the cook would make her another breakfast, hopefully scrambled eggs.
