1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Montanan’s Death In Mexican Jail Protested By U.S. Ambassador April 30, 1992
2 CONT
2 CONT MEXICO CITY (AP) _ The U.S. ambassador has filed a protest over the death of a Montana lawyer at the hands of police in Ciudad Juarez, near the Texas border, an embassy spokeswoman said Thursday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence Edwards Eck, 48, died in Ciudad Juarez’ General Hospital on Jan. 23 from internal injuries, including several burst organs.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had been brutally beaten, forensic doctor Enrique Silva told El Universal newspaper. Silva conducted the autopsy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eck, of Bozeman, is the son of Dorothy Eck, a three-term Montana state senator. His sister, Diana, is a professor of religion at Harvard University.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy Eck told a Bozeman newspaper in January that her son, a lawyer, had been trying to set up an international trade business in Juarez and was living in a hotel there.
2 CONT
2 CONT She said a disagreement with the hotel owner ended up in a Mexican court, where he ″tried to inform the Mexican judge about his rights to bring witnesses,″ she said. He was cited for contempt of court and arrested Jan. 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time after that he telephoned relatives to say he had been beaten while in jail, said Ann Sheridan of the U.S. Consulate General in Ciudad Juarez.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy Eck said in January that if she had pressed Mexican authorities to investigate the blows her son suffered, she probably would have had difficulties getting his body promptly returned to the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jennifer Gallenkamp, a spokeswoman for Ambassador John Negroponte, declined to comment further on the case.
2 CONT
2 CONT ″The embassy is in contact with the highest-level officials regarding Mr. Eck’s death and closely following the investigation,″ she said.
